 \begin{abstract}
-Black hole ringdown carries information about the remnant mass and spin and provides a basis for tests of general relativity. Space-based detectors offer access to the ringdowns of massive black hole binaries in the millihertz band. Data gaps near the onset of black hole ringdown can obscure much of its rapidly decaying signal. We present DenoiseGapFiller (DGF), a neural network that jointly denoises and reconstructs ringdown waveforms using complementary time--frequency and temporal representations. We evaluate DGF on 6,000 independent simulated LISA $A/E$ observations formed by adding stationary Gaussian instrument noise to ringdown signals with gaps spanning approximately 6\%--10\% of each record. For network signal-to-noise ratios $\rho\ge10$, the mean direct noise-weighted mismatch is $5.10\times10^{-3}$ at fixed arrival time and phase. The corresponding mean over all 6,000 test signals is $8.21\times10^{-3}$. Across the full test population, the mean absolute analytic-phase error at each channel's clean-signal envelope peak is below $4^\circ$. These results demonstrate recovery of damped oscillations across data gaps in the simulated source population.
